lled-cepevorous-naga] ('The plan-review gate is reached through three surfaces: the CLI (`rebar review-p') names no VERIFIED upstream prerequisite: P neither declares `depends_on -> skilled-cepevorous-naga` nor is blocked by it. Add a `depends_on` edge to skilled-cepevorous-naga, ensure skilled-cepevorous-naga `blocks` this ticket, or remove the citation. Until then the cited symbol is grounded as normal (fails closed)."],"finding":"Acceptance-criteria / definition-of-done quality issues.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f607dd457c67f9209","impact":0.4,"is_primary":null,"location":"","norm_id":"ned06ebc801a522e3","priority":0.4,"reason":null,"recalled":false,"scenarios":[],"severity":"minor","suggested_fix":"Split compound criteria, replace subjective terms with observable outcomes, and state how each is verified.","tier":"DET","validity":1.0,"verification":null},{"block_threshold":0.6,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] State whether `_review_plan_body` always forwards `engine=` to `rebar.llm.review_plan`, and say that every test stand-in replacing `review_plan` must therefore accept `engine` (or `**kwargs`).","cohort":["COH","E1","E2","E6","F1","T2","T4","T5b","T5e","T7","T9"],"criteria":["E2"],"decision":"indeterminate","decision_margin":-0.6,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["\"tests/unit/test_gate_admission_09da.py (`_review_plan_body` caller, if its stand-in needs `engine`)\"","\"`_review_plan_body` (new keyword-only `engine: str | None = None` ...), which passes it to `rebar.llm.review_plan`\""],"finding":"The Scope bullet for `tests/unit/test_gate_admission_09da.py` is conditional ('if its stand-in needs `engine`'). The plan also does not say whether `_review_plan_body` always forwards `engine=` to `rebar.llm.review_plan` or only when it is not None. That choice decides whether existing stand-ins with a fixed signature break.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"fadba0da8b344aa4c","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Scope: tests/unit/test_gate_admission_09da.py; Approach MCP bullet (`_review_plan_body` keyword-only `engine`)","norm_id":"n7b59cbbe6550dfb2","priority":0.0,"reason":"no-verification","recalled":false,"scenarios":["The body unconditionally calls `review_plan(..., engine=engine)` and a monkeypatched stand-in lacking an `engine` parameter raises TypeError, so an unrelated gate-admission test turns red."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"Specify that `_review_plan_body` always passes `engine=engine`, and that every stand-in for `rebar.llm.review_plan` (in `test_mcp_gate_async.py` and `test_gate_admission_09da.py`) is updated to accept `engine=None`. Drop the 'if' from the Scope bullet.","tier":"LLM","validity":0.0,"verification":null},{"block_threshold":0.6,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Say that the `None` case is tested at the tool level (`review_plan_start(engine=None)` gets the same `job_id` as `engine=\"pipeline\"` under default config), and that `_plan_review_variant` receives only resolved names.","cohort":["COH","E1","E2","E6","F1","T2","T4","T5b","T5e","T7","T9"],"criteria":["E2","COH"],"decision":"indeterminate","decision_margin":-0.6,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Approach: \"Each tool resolves the engine ONCE ... and passes the resolved name to both `_plan_review_variant(ticket_id, source, engine)`\"","Testing: \"`_plan_review_variant` differs between \\\"pipeline\\\" and \\\"agentic\\\". A `None` argument resolves to the configured default before keying.\""],"finding":"The dedup test bullet says 'A `None` argument resolves to the configured default before keying.' The Approach, however, has the tool resolve first and pass only the resolved name to `_plan_review_variant(ticket_id, source, engine)`. It is unclear whether `_plan_review_variant` must itself accept and resolve `None`, or whether the test belongs at the tool level.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"fb0c1b6ddd5d23406","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Testing: Dedup key bullet vs Approach: MCP bullet","norm_id":"n6a8f48d495337c6d","priority":0.0,"reason":"no-verification","recalled":false,"scenarios":["The executor adds config resolution inside `_plan_review_variant` as well, giving two resolution points that could drift.","Alternatively the test calls `_plan_review_variant(..., None)` and gets `;engine=None`."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"Reword the bullet: \"`review_plan_start(ticket, engine=None)` under default config dedups to the same job as `engine=\\\"pipeline\\\"`; `_plan_review_variant` is only ever called with a resolved engine name.\"","tier":"LLM","validity":0.0,"verification":null},{"block_threshold":0.95,"blocking_enabled":false,"checklist_item":"- [ ] State that the engine description goes in a later docstring paragraph (first paragraph unchanged), and add the mcp-reference generator's `--check` (or its drift test) to AC3.","cohort":["COH","E1","E2","E6","F1","T2","T4","T5b","T5e","T7","T9"],"criteria":["E6","T7"],"decision":"indeterminate","decision_margin":-0.95,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["\"`docs/mcp-reference.md` lists only names and first-paragraph summaries, which this change keeps, so it does not change.\"","AC3 names only `gen_cli_help.py --check`, `gen_cli_reference.py --check` and the MCP golden test."],"finding":"The plan claims `docs/mcp-reference.md` will not change because it lists only first-paragraph summaries. It does not tell the executor to put the new engine wording after the first paragraph of the `review_plan` and `review_plan_start` docstrings. No AC checks that `docs/mcp-reference.md` stays in sync.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f15de1715be2a6faf","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach: Generated artifacts bullet; Acceptance Criteria 3","norm_id":"n2795998c84f268aa","priority":0.0,"reason":"no-verification","recalled":false,"scenarios":["The executor adds 'engine selects pipeline/agentic' to the docstring summary line, so `docs/mcp-reference.md` drifts and the claimed no-op is false. That would be caught only incidentally by `make verify`, if at all."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"","tier":"LLM","validity":0.0,"verification":null},{"block_threshold":0.7,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] State whether the gate job index is in-memory or persisted. If persisted, note that old-key in-flight jobs are not deduped after the upgrade, and either accept that explicitly or treat a key without an engine as `engine=pipeline` when matching.","cohort":["COH","E1","E2","E6","F1","T2","T4","T5b","T5e","T7","T9"],"criteria":["T9","T4"],"decision":"indeterminate","decision_margin":-0.7,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["\"`_plan_review_variant(ticket_id, source, engine)`, which appends `;engine=<resolved>` to the key\"","Testing refers to \"the gate job index\" without stating its lifetime."],"finding":"Changing the dedup key format (adding `;engine=<resolved>`) could matter if the gate job index outlives a server restart. A job started before the upgrade would carry the old key, so a same-engine start after the upgrade would not dedup against it and would launch a second concurrent review on the same ticket. The plan does not say whether the index is in-memory only or persisted.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f9070438f33675c8c","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach: MCP bullet (`_plan_review_variant` appends `;engine=<resolved>`)","norm_id":"n596ef5d8f8660344","priority":0.0,"reason":"no-verification","recalled":false,"scenarios":["An in-flight pipeline job keyed `<ticket>;source=...` exists in a persisted index. After the upgrade, `review_plan_start(engine='pipeline')` computes `...;engine=pipeline`, misses it, and starts a duplicate run writing a competing REVIEW_RESULT sidecar."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"","tier":"LLM","validity":0.0,"verification":null},{"block_threshold":0.9,"blocking_enabled":false,"checklist_item":"- [ ] State the disposition of the unknown-engine arm: a permanent/non-retryable caller error (not WAIT_AND_RETRY). Say what the MCP sync and async tools surface to the client (raised tool error vs structured failure payload), and pin it in test_plan_review_engine_surfaces.py.","cohort":["E3","E5","F4","G5","T6","necessity","project.engine-layer-enforcement","project.failure-disposition-contract","project.net-mechanism","project.portability"],"criteria":["project.failure-disposition-contract"],"decision":"indeterminate","decision_margin":-0.9,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["\"An explicit unknown value raises `ConfigError` from that resolution, before any job is created or LLM call made.\"","Congestion is described as returning \"the same `_structured_llm_failure` payload\", but no comparable caller-visible shape is stated for the bogus-engine path.","CLI `--engine bogus` exits 2 via argparse, a different disposition token from the MCP raise; the plan does not reconcile the two."],"finding":"The plan adds a new rejection path on the MCP `review_plan`/`review_plan_start` tools: an explicit unknown `engine` raises `ConfigError` before any job is created. It names only the exception type. It does not state the disposition with an explicit word (a permanent, non-retryable caller error, versus the retryable exit-11 congestion path). It also does not say what an MCP client actually sees. Since `ConfigError` is not an `LLMError`, the sync tool would not return its `_structured_llm_failure` payload; instead a raised tool error would surface.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f2cca6a214d164562","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach → MCP: \"An explicit unknown value raises `ConfigError` from that resolution, before any job is created or LLM call made.\"","norm_id":"nd2e3b92cd2b00441","priority":0.0,"reason":"no-verification","recalled":false,"scenarios":["An MCP client agent receives a raised ConfigError from review_plan_start(engine='bogus'). The plan does not say it is permanent, so the client may treat it like GATE_CONGESTED and retry in a loop."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"Add one inline sentence, e.g.: \"An unknown engine is a permanent caller error: the CLI rejects it at argparse (exit 2), and both MCP tools raise ConfigError (surfaced as an MCP tool error, not a retryable GATE_CONGESTED/structured LLM failure); it is never retryable.\"","tier":"LLM","validity":0.0,"verification":null},{"block_threshold":0.95,"blocking_enabled":false,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Specify that the None-default dedup test drives `review_plan_start(..., engine=None)` and asserts it shares a job_id with an explicit `engine=\"pipeline\"` start. Do not have it call `_plan_review_variant` with None.","cohort":["E3","E5","F4","G5","T6","necessity","project.engine-layer-enforcement","project.failure-disposition-contract","project.net-mechanism","project.portability"],"criteria":["E5"],"decision":"indeterminate","decision_margin":-0.95,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Approach: \"Each tool resolves the engine ONCE (`resolve_plan_review_engine(engine)` against the server's config) and passes the resolved name to both `_plan_review_variant(ticket_id, source, engine)`\"","Testing: \"A `None` argument resolves to the configured default before keying.\""],"finding":"The dedup test line 'A `None` argument resolves to the configured default before keying' leaves the tested layer unclear. The Approach says the tool resolves the engine once and passes the resolved name to `_plan_review_variant`. The test line, however, reads as if `_plan_review_variant` itself accepts None and resolves it, which would need config access inside the key helper. Because the oracle is ambiguous, the implementer may test, or build, the wrong layer.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f70f8029b4646d493","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Testing → Dedup key: \"A `None` argument resolves to the configured default before keying.\"","norm_id":"na1f02d8eb41ee85d","priority":0.0,"reason":"no-verification","recalled":false,"scenarios":["The implementer adds None-handling inside `_plan_review_variant` to satisfy the test. This duplicates resolution and weakens the 'resolve ONCE' guarantee that keeps the key and the run in agreement."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"Reword to: \"`review_plan_start(..., engine=None)` under a pipeline-configured repo returns the same job_id as a concurrent `engine=\"pipeline\"` start (resolution happens in the tool before keying).\"","tier":"LLM","validity":0.0,"verification":null},{"block_threshold":0.95,"blocking_enabled":false,"checklist_item":"- [ ] State whether the `_plan_review_variant` key is persisted or parsed anywhere beyond in-process dedup. If it is, acknowledge the old-format keys (or confirm none survive a restart).","cohort":["E3","E5","F4","G5","T6","necessity","project.engine-layer-enforcement","project.failure-disposition-contract","project.net-mechanism","project.portability"],"criteria":["E3"],"decision":"indeterminate","decision_margin":-0.95,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["\"which appends `;engine=<resolved>` to the key\"","Scope lists only \"tests/unit/test_mcp_llm_dedup.py (variant key gains `;engine=pipeline`)\" as a dependent of the key format.","Testing refers to \"the gate job index\", suggesting job state may be indexed."],"finding":"The plan changes the dedup variant key format by appending `;engine=<resolved>`. The only dependent it acknowledges is `tests/unit/test_mcp_llm_dedup.py`. It does not say whether the variant string is persisted, for example in the gate job index that `gate_status` reads. Nor does it say whether any reader parses the variant string. If the key is persisted, a job started before upgrade, keyed without `;engine=`, would no longer dedup with an identical start after upgrade.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f7a6ad01b71b5cf85","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach → MCP: \"`_plan_review_variant(ticket_id, source, engine)`, which appends `;engine=<resolved>` to the key\"","norm_id":"n6baf8adca81a92bd","priority":0.0,"reason":"no-verification","recalled":false,"scenarios":["A server restarts mid-review after upgrade. A new pipeline start on the same ticket does not attach to the in-flight pre-upgrade job and launches a duplicate review."],"severity":null,"suggested_fix":"","tier":"LLM","validity":0.0,"verification":null},{"block_threshold":0.6,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] State that `review_plan_start` resolves the engine inside the `anyio.to_thread.run_sync` worker (in the same lambda that builds the variant and the work closure), not in the coroutine body.","cohort":["G6"],"criteria":["G6"],"decision":"indeterminate","decision_margin":-0.6,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan: \"Each tool resolves the engine ONCE (`resolve_plan_review_engine(engine)` against the server's config)\"","_mcp_llm.py:288 \"# Basis resolution and index writes block, so keep them off the event loop.\" and :291-292 \"The material read for the dedup variant is a store read too, so it runs in the worker thread\"","plan_review/__init__.py:315 `return _config.compose_config(repo_root).verify.plan_review_engine`"],"finding":"The plan says each MCP tool 'resolves the engine ONCE (`resolve_plan_review_engine(engine)` against the server's config)' but not where `review_plan_start` does it. When `engine` is None, `resolve_plan_review_engine` calls `compose_config(...)`, which is blocking config-file I/O. The async starter's existing rule is that blocking start work runs inside `anyio.to_thread.run_sync`, not on the event loop. A natural implementation that resolves the engine in the coroutine body before the `await` would break that rule.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f8be4aa73864eb8b7","impact":0.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Approach → MCP bullet; src/rebar/_mcp_llm.py review_plan_start (lines 287-304)","norm_id":"n23ef3c0901aff73f","priority":0.0,"reason":"no-verification","recalled":false,"scenarios":["An implementer